1
00:00:00,867 --> 00:00:04,467
Your AI agents work in parallel.
You can’t.

2
00:00:05,533 --> 00:00:06,867
Who Routes the Agents?

3
00:00:06,867 --> 00:00:12,467
Super-agent, a design by
DeepSeek’s Harness team

4
00:00:13,533 --> 00:00:17,467
Each session knows one task.
You must know them all.

5
00:00:17,867 --> 00:00:21,800
In the authors’ experience,
you can follow about 3–5.

6
00:00:22,200 --> 00:00:26,800
You switch, prompt each run,
wait, and keep it all running.

7
00:00:27,867 --> 00:00:31,467
You’ve quietly become their router.

8
00:00:32,867 --> 00:00:37,467
All in one session: it remembers,
but is serial and muddled.

9
00:00:37,867 --> 00:00:42,467
One per event: fast, but disconnected,
and all reply to you.

10
00:00:42,867 --> 00:00:46,133
Continuity or speed: must we pick one?

11
00:00:48,200 --> 00:00:51,800
Super-agent keeps both, on two levels.

12
00:00:52,200 --> 00:00:56,133
Above: one long-lived
Router hears every channel.

13
00:00:56,533 --> 00:01:00,467
It hands each task over whole
and never does the work.

14
00:01:00,867 --> 00:01:05,467
Why? Every task passes here;
it must never be the bottleneck.

15
00:01:06,200 --> 00:01:10,800
Below: each task gets its own Worker,
context and tools.

16
00:01:11,200 --> 00:01:15,133
Workers report up.
Only the Router answers you.

17
00:01:17,533 --> 00:01:21,800
It listens for events,
so no one has to prompt each run.

18
00:01:22,533 --> 00:01:27,467
By design: rules drop most noise;
a model judges the rest.

19
00:01:30,867 --> 00:01:35,467
Can one Router keep up?
Waiting events go in together.

20
00:01:35,867 --> 00:01:40,467
Long input, short output:
merging events adds little time.

21
00:01:42,200 --> 00:01:46,800
Context is a cache: a summary in view,
a pointer to the rest.

22
00:01:47,533 --> 00:01:52,133
Asked in another channel?
It follows IDs back, hop by hop.

23
00:01:54,867 --> 00:01:58,467
Follow-ups in a Thread go
straight to its Worker.

24
00:01:58,867 --> 00:02:02,800
Not faster tasks:
about 23% fewer Router decisions.

25
00:02:03,200 --> 00:02:07,800
Jobs run in the background;
new input never waits on them.

26
00:02:13,533 --> 00:02:17,800
Slowest tasks: about half
the time of a single session.

27
00:02:18,200 --> 00:02:22,467
Typical tasks: only ~10% faster
than the stronger baseline.

28
00:02:22,867 --> 00:02:26,800
Left to choose,
the single session never delegated.

29
00:02:27,200 --> 00:02:31,800
Two levels make delegation structural,
at 2–4× the tokens.

30
00:02:32,867 --> 00:02:37,133
Proposed: a cloud loop that
works while your laptop sleeps.

31
00:02:39,200 --> 00:02:44,467
A watchdog checks progress,
not just that it’s alive.

32
00:02:45,533 --> 00:02:50,467
Open risk: a hijacked loop could
reach all connected machines.

33
00:02:50,867 --> 00:02:55,133
Shard the Router and continuity
trades against speed again.

34
00:02:55,867 --> 00:03:01,133
Many tasks still need you midway:
how to step in is open.

35
00:03:05,867 --> 00:03:10,467
None of this is new theory:
it is classic systems design.

36
00:03:10,867 --> 00:03:15,467
As models need less help,
your attention becomes the limit.

37
00:03:15,867 --> 00:03:19,800
So people move up:
to decisions, and to sign-off.

38
00:03:20,533 --> 00:03:25,133
Your agents work in parallel.
By design, you don’t have to.

39
00:03:26,867 --> 00:03:31,633
Source: “Exploring Super-agent Design:
Towards a More Autonomous Agent System”

40
00:03:31,633 --> 00:03:34,133
